PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.3/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a strong, polished set with consistently clean pictures and hand-checked unique solutions. Every level has a clear stated idea, and the trap rate is 0 throughout. Chapter structure is tidy: teach, practise, twist, boss, breather, finale. Casual win rates hit the studio targets. The last third averages about 62%, no spike is large, and there is never a run of easy levels. Weak points:
1. Several chapters turn one trick into repetition. Colour chapter 3 is mostly 'a row fills exactly because different colours touch'. The smudged and cracked chapters are mostly 'solve the exact row, then read the hidden cell from its column'.
2. Mid-game picture quality is uneven: L13, L18, L24, L47 and L60 are hard to read as their titles, or leave a lot of dead paper.
3. Late 'practise' levels often reuse the same deduction as their 'teach' level, with a bigger board but no new insight.
4. Boss levels in chapters 1 and 2 are too gentle.
5. The Observatory and Grand Exhibit chapters stack many 55-60% levels back to back, with little relief apart from the breathers.
6. A few mechanic tags and levels don't match the board: L102 is tagged color-cross on a 2-colour board, and L60's 'Dolphin Medallion' reads as a plain ring. Curve: Chapter 1 (L1-12) is a good ramp. It teaches fill, exact-fit, overlap and X-marks one at a time, with a twist at L5 and L8, a boss at L6, a breather at L7 and a finale at L12. However, the boss (83% casual) is barely harder than L5 (88%). Chapter 2 (L13-24) introduces 'reach' cleanly at L13. After that, five of the next ten levels differ only in the picture: 'the perch/floor row fixes the columns'. Casual win rates sit at 84-89% until L18, a 79% dip. L19 is a good breather.

Chapters 3-4 add colour and voids correctly: a teach pair, then practise, twist, combine and finale. The colour chapter (L25-36) is the repetitive one. Seven of its twelve levels have the intent 'a row fills exactly because touching colours share no gap', so its 'twist' and 'practise' levels feel alike. The void chapter is the best-paced of the set. Its voids as shortened lines, mid-canvas voids and the void+colour bridge (L45) all earn an aha.

Mosaic Hall (stamp) is well introduced but thin as a mechanic. Several levels use a stamp only to disambiguate one column. L55 is the standout, and L60 is a 15x15 grind.

Conservatory (colour-cross) has a proper teach-to-finale arc. The smudged and cracked chapters then rely on one pattern: find the exact row, then read the hidden cell from its column. The 'bound the ?' idea in particular appears about 8 times. Difficulty from L81 on runs at roughly 55-65% casual, which is on target. It is a plateau, though. L89, L99, L111 and L112 sit at 54-59% as 'practise' or 'twist' levels with the same insight as their neighbours, so players feel fatigue rather than escalation. The breathers (L91, L103, L115) are correctly easier. Each late chapter needs one truly new idea before its boss, not another combine. L97 (an 11-pass chain) is the best late aha. The finales (L84, L96, L108, L120) are all good.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L6 (7/10): Casual 83% is barely below L5 (88%). The boss reads as one more practise level, with 11 lines open and a long chain of X's but no multi-step surprise. → Remove one easy opening line (e.g. change row 7's clue so it is no longer a near-full row) so the claw chain needs 2 extra passes. Aim for about 75% casual.
L13 (7/10): The picture is a scatter of two small caps and a pole. Rows 1-5 on the right are mostly dead paper, and the 'toadstool' doesn't read. → Redraw as a clear mushroom cluster with caps spanning more of the width, so the empty top-right area carries ink.
L18 (7/10): The deer is not readable as a deer. The picture is a diamond with antler fragments in row 1. The 79% casual rate is a dip compared with the neighbouring levels at 84-86%. → Redraw with a legible profile, with antlers connected to the head and four distinct legs. Keep the 5 6 / 4 6 anchors.
L24 (7/10): The heron is not readable. The left half is a thin neck and the right a block, with the largest empty areas in the chapter. It fails as a 'Masterwork' reveal. → Redraw a recognisable heron and reflection, using the space between the columns. Keep the row 11 waterline anchor.
L28 (7/10): The insight 'read columns, edge cells are the teal' is fine. But 83% casual with 18 lines open at start is just walk-through work, with no step that surprises. → Make row 3 non-exact and swap one teal cell so a column's teal run must be placed against a red run, which forces a colour-order inference.
L29 (7/10): The 'twist' is another exact-fit row with touching colours, the same idea as L25 and L26. It isn't a twist. → Replace the gold/brown row with a row where touching colours leave one spare cell (e.g. a colour-order case), or move this to practise and add a real twist.
L30 (7/10): The boss is two exact rows (row 2 and row 8) plus two 8s. That is a repeat of exact-fit with no chain. The jar's lid and body are blocky. → Make row 8 non-exact (add a spare cell) so the lid and body depend on each other.
L32 (7/10): The idea, 'stem first, exact column', is the third level in a row built on a single full line. The flowerpot has a lot of empty paper in the top half. → Shift the pot and stem so the stem column is not exact, or fill the top with a bigger bloom.
L35 (7/10): The picture is mostly a rim row and a weave row. The apples at the top are hard to read, and the colour-order insight repeats L27/L29. → Enlarge the apples and give each a distinct size so the colour deduction differs per column.
L47 (7/10): The 'Bronze Mina' picture doesn't read as the curator cat. The grid has an awkward plinth and a diffuse head. Casual 75% is a step down from L46 and the idea overlaps L46. → Redraw with cat ears and a face, and use a plinth that isn't one wide block.
L52 (7/10): The stamp pins one cell of a single stem. It feels like decoration, and the 'sage 1' is the whole insight. Casual 77% is the lowest in the early chapter. → Add a second stamp that decides a different ambiguity, e.g. the fruit's edge.
L56 (7/10): The toucan is not readable, and the lower-left bird shape is mostly empty. The stamp is trivial (a lone 1 at the corner). → Redraw with a clear beak and body, and move the stamp to a cell that resolves a real ambiguity.
L60 (7/10): The 'Dolphin Medallion' is a plain ring with a diffuse interior, and the dolphin doesn't read. A 15x15 with 56 moves and 9 passes is grindy for one stamp deduction. → Draw a clear dolphin in the ring, and add a second stamp so the chain splits into two shorter ones.
L64 (7/10): The can is clumsy: a rectangle body with handle dots. 74% casual is low for a practise level, and the insight repeats L62. → Add a distinct spout/handle shape and trim one exact row, or move this later in the chapter.
L68 (7/10): The intent mentions only the void ('nine cells'), not colour-cross, so the combine isn't demonstrated. The ladybird is hard to read. → Rewrite the board so a colour-cross cell is needed after the void trim. Make the spots clearer.
L75 (7/10): The hidden row has only a stud pattern, with every cell set by its column. It is mechanical busywork (19 looks) with no aha beyond L73/L74. → Make the hidden row asymmetrical so one cell needs the row total or a neighbour check.
L76 (7/10): The koi picture is crowded and the three-colour fill in the middle is a blob. The insight repeats the previous smudged level. → Simplify to a clear koi silhouette and add a dedicated column-4/7 deduction.
L77 (7/10): 69% casual is the lowest of the early lantern levels, and the 'twist' is just flipping rows to columns. Hard but not a new idea. → Add a case where two smudged columns each depend on the other, or soften difficulty to 75%.
L78 (7/10): The boss is no harder in idea than L77. The picture reads as two unrelated shapes (moon and lantern). → Merge the moon and lantern into one composition with a shared chain.
L80 (7/10): The parasol is symmetrical and the smudged column adds little. 76% casual follows the 89% breather with no ramp. → Break the symmetry (a different left/right canopy) so column 4 needs real cross-checking.
L81 (7/10): Casual 61% for a 'combine' in the middle of the chapter. 'Nothing in the column is given' plus 28 looks is a big step up. → Add a second exact row or a stamp in the hidden column to cut ambiguity.
L82 (7/10): Only 3 passes but 59% casual. The edge cells in the hidden row (columns 1 and 13) risk a guess when the player isn't sure whether the row touches the edges. → Give the edge columns a clue that forces the answer (e.g. a clue of 2 that touches the hidden row) so there is no choice.
L83 (7/10): The pinwheel's two halves are symmetrical, so the chain solved on one side is repeated on the other. 60% casual with low aha. → Make the vanes different lengths to avoid the mirror.
L86 (7/10): The intent admits 'the crack costs two extra passes'. The heart cut-out and the handle fight for attention, so it teaches two things at once. → Remove the heart cut-out and keep a plain mug with one cracked row.
L87 (7/10): 66% casual with two ideas (colour + crack), and the vase is a smudge of colours with a hard-to-read chip. → Use the teapot-style single-colour vase first, or reduce clutter in the shoulder.

